vtsatip - SAT>IP plugin for VDR with the behaviour of vtuner-ng
================================================================

What is it?
-----------
A VDR plugin which builds the SAT>IP client of vtuner-ng (the "satip" daemon)
directly into VDR. The vtunerc kernel module and the userspace daemon are no
longer needed; nothing has to be recompiled after a kernel update.

The plugin is deliberately NOT a variation of the plugin by FireFlyVDR but
follows the control flow of vtuner-ng (the split into devices, tuner, section
filter etc. and the mapping channels.conf -> SAT>IP URL come from the satip
plugin, GPLv2):

 * One persistent RTSP session per device. Only the first tuning does
   OPTIONS + SETUP (pids=none) + PLAY. Every further switch to another
   transponder is exactly one PLAY with the tuning parameters and the
   complete PID list.
 * Same transponder: no RTSP traffic at all, the lock is kept.
 * PID changes are sent immediately as addpids/delpids (no 250 ms timer).
 * PIDs 0,16,17,18,19,20 (PAT, NIT, SDT, EIT, ...) are always requested.
 * "Lock" is declared as soon as the first real TS packet arrives (like in
   the kernel module); the plugin does not wait for RTCP or DESCRIBE.
 * SetChannelDevice() returns immediately, it does not wait for the tuner.
 * Packets of the old transponder which arrive during a retune are dropped,
   the buffers are flushed.
 * RTP is received in a thread of its own, the socket is drained completely,
   SCHED_FIFO (lowest realtime priority) is tried like in the vtuner daemon.
 * Session keep-alive with OPTIONS; the tuner rebuilds itself after errors.

Installation
------------
  cd VDR-source-directory/PLUGINS/src
  tar xzf vdr-vtsatip-<version>.tar.gz ; ln -s vdr-plugin-vtsatip-<version> vtsatip
  cd ../.. && make plugins && make install-plugins    (or make install-lib in the plugin)

Only VDR >= 2.4 and libpthread are needed (no curl, no XML library).

Usage / switching from vtuner-ng
--------------------------------
  vtuner-ng:   satip -s 192.168.1.20 -D DVBS2 -f 1 (one daemon per device)
  vtsatip:     -P "vtsatip -s 192.168.1.20 -d 2 -D DVBS,DVBS2 -f 1,2"

  -s HOST[:PORT][|CAPS][|NAME]
                   SAT>IP server, may be given several times (see UPnP)
  -u MODE          UPnP discovery: 0 = off, 1 = on (default: on if no -s is given)
  -d NUM           number of VDR devices = simultaneous streams (default 1)
  -D LIST          DVBS,DVBS2,DVBT,DVBT2,DVBC (default: all)
  -f LIST          fixed server frontend per device (0 = the server decides)
  -c CAIDS         CI extension: CA IDs of each CI slot of the server (see below)
  -i LIST          CI slot (1..4) per VDR device, 0 = none, e.g. -i 1,0
  -S SIDS          restrict the CI slots to service IDs, e.g. -S '4911,5301;'
  -Q               do NOT restart a stream when its CI state changes
  -T MODE          unicast (default), multicast or tcp
  -b ADDRESS       local interface (IP) used to join multicast groups
  -p START-STOP    RTP/RTCP port range (default 45000-47000)
  -r BYTES         size of the RTP receive buffer (default 2 MiB)
  -a               request the whole transponder (pids=all), needs a lot of bandwidth
  -A               start with all devices detached (see SVDRP ATTA/DETA)
  -N               do not tear the stream down when a device becomes idle
  -I SEC           release the stream of a device that was not used for SEC
                   seconds (5-3600, default 20)
  -R               do not try realtime priority
  -t 1             tracing: RTSP traffic and tuning in the syslog

Important differences to vtuner-ng
----------------------------------
 * The satellite position (src=) no longer comes from the DiSEqC message VDR
   sent to the virtual frontend. Like in the satip plugin it is read from the
   description of the source in sources.conf (leading number):
        S19.2E  1 Astra 19.2E        -> src=1
        S13.0E  2 Hotbird            -> src=2
   Without a number src=1 is used. A source whose description starts with "0"
   is not served by this plugin.
 * VDR's diseqc.conf / LNB settings have no meaning for these devices (the
   SAT>IP server does that).
 * There is no /dev/dvb/adapterX any more; programs other than VDR cannot use
   the device.

Server discovery with UPnP (since 0.3.0)
----------------------------------------
Without -s the plugin looks for SAT>IP servers itself (SSDP M-SEARCH to
239.255.255.250:1900, repeated every 60 s, plus NOTIFY alive/byebye). Name,
RTSP port (header X-SATIP-RTSP-Port) and capabilities (X_SATIPCAP, e.g.
DVBS2-4,DVBT2-2) are read from the device description. No XML or HTTP
library is needed.

  vdr -P 'vtsatip -d 4'                            everything automatic
  vdr -P 'vtsatip -d 2 -s 192.168.1.20'            fixed server, no discovery
  vdr -P 'vtsatip -d 4 -u 1 -s 192.168.1.20|DVBS2-2|Octonet -s 192.168.1.30:8554|DVBT2-4'

CAPS gives the number of frontends per kind of reception, like UPnP does.
Without CAPS the server has any number of frontends for the systems allowed by
-D (so a single -s behaves as before). Manual entries win over the same
device found by UPnP and are never removed by "byebye".

CI: The CI extension (slot assignment) is only used on servers which have a
CI. UPnP servers are recognised by their model, e.g. OctopusNet/Octonet and
minisatip; a manual server is assumed to have a CI unless NOCI is in its list
(-s 192.168.1.30|DVBS2-4,NOCI). A UPnP server which is not recognised cannot
be forced; configure it with -s and CAPS instead.

Satellite position: after CAPS a list of positions can follow, separated by
':' (spelled like in VDR's sources.conf), e.g.
  -s 192.168.1.20|DVBS2-4:S19.2E,S13E|Octonet
The server is then only used for channels of these positions. This is useful
when several servers are connected to different satellites. Without a filter
the server takes all positions.

Every VDR device (-d) picks a server with a free frontend for the kind of
reception (sat/terr/cable) when it is switched and stays there as long as it
is used on that server. If everything is busy VDR uses another device. A
device that is not used any more (no recording, no live view, no tuning) for
20 seconds (-I) gives its frontend back to the server (since 0.7.5; before
that only the EPG scanner's pause did this). Switching back within that time
is as fast as ever. A source is only available if a known
server supplies the matching system. Be careful with -f (fixed frontends) and
several servers: the number applies to all servers.

SVDRP: plug vtsatip SCAN    starts a new search at once
       plug vtsatip INFO    shows servers (busy/free) and devices
femon and other tools: the device type (DeviceType) is the delivery system of
the last tuning, e.g. "DVB-S2", "DVB-S", "DVB-T2" or "DVB-C", like with a real
DVB card (since 0.7.3). Before the first tuning it is the best enabled system.
The device name still shows "vtSAT>IP <n> (<server>)".
Firewall: UDP 1900 (multicast) and the RTP ports (-p) must be reachable.
Security (since 0.7.2): the device description is only fetched from the host
that sent the SSDP announcement, i.e. the host in LOCATION must be the sender's
IP address. Announcements that point elsewhere are ignored. A server with
several addresses that announces a LOCATION with another address than the one
it sends from must be entered by hand with -s. Discovery can be switched off
with -u 0.

CI/CAM (since 0.2.0)
--------------------
The SAT>IP extension "x_pmt/x_ci" is supported (e.g. Digital Devices
OctopusNet, minisatip with CA): the server decrypts with its own CI slot and
VDR receives the finished stream.

  vdr -P 'vtsatip -s 192.168.1.20 -d 2 -c "0x1830,0x1843;0x0500" -i 1,2'

  -c  CA IDs per slot: slot 1 can do 0x1830 and 0x1843, slot 2 can do 0x0500.
  -i  device 0 uses slot 1, device 1 uses slot 2. Without -i slot n belongs
      to device n-1.

How it works: if a channel is encrypted and one of its CA IDs matches the slot
of the device, the plugin finds the PMT PID of the service in the PAT (the PAT
is always in the stream, no VDR internals are needed), remembers it per
transponder/service and sends it as &x_pmt=<pids>&x_ci=<slot>. The PMT PID is
also added to the PID list. Further services of the same transponder are added
to the x_pmt list (several services per CAM, if the CAM can do that). When a
channel is used for the first time the PMT PID is unknown: the stream runs
unencrypted for a moment and is then restarted once with x_pmt automatically.
After that the PID comes from the cache.

The restart is necessary because some servers only attach the CI to streams
which were started with x_pmt and release it only when the session is torn
down. Servers which do not need that can be run without the restart using -Q.
Devices with a CI only take channels whose CA IDs the slot can do; VDR does
not use an own CAM plugin for such devices (HasInternalCam).

Service IDs per CI slot: with -S '4911,5301;' (or in the setup menu) a slot
can be restricted to certain service IDs, like in vtuner-ng. Slots without a
list take every service their CA IDs can decrypt. Useful if a slot only has a
subscription module for a few channels.

Multicast (since 0.5.0)
-----------------------
  vdr -P 'vtsatip -d 4 -T multicast -b 192.168.1.10'

In SETUP the plugin sends "Transport: RTP/AVP;multicast" without a
destination; the server answers with group, port, TTL and optionally a source
(SSM). The plugin joins the group (IGMP), reads RTP and RTCP from there and
leaves it when the stream is torn down. Control (PLAY, addpids/delpids,
keep-alive) still runs over RTSP.

If a server does not offer multicast or refuses it (e.g. 461) the plugin
automatically uses unicast and remembers that for this server.

Advantage: several clients can receive the same transponder without the
server sending it several times. Requirement: IGMP snooping in the switch,
otherwise the whole network (especially WLAN) is flooded with the stream. -b
is needed if the computer has several network interfaces. In the setup menu:
"Transport mode". The setting applies to new sessions.

RTP over TCP (since 0.6.0)
--------------------------
  vdr -P 'vtsatip -T tcp'

SETUP contains "Transport: RTP/AVP/TCP;unicast;interleaved=0-1". The server
then sends RTP (channel 0) and RTCP (channel 1) as "$" frames over the same
TCP connection as the RTSP responses; no UDP ports are needed (firewall, NAT,
VPN). Drawback: TCP retransmits can add latency and a lost packet stalls the
stream. UDP is better in a normal LAN. If a server answers with an error or
with UDP, UDP unicast is used for it automatically. In the setup menu:
"Transport mode" = TCP.

Whole transponder (since 0.7.0)
-------------------------------
With -a (setup menu: "Request all PIDs") the plugin sends pids=all instead of
a PID list. The server then streams the complete transponder (many Mbit/s),
VDR picks what it needs, and PID changes cause no RTSP traffic at all. It is
meant for recording everything of a transponder or for testing. The setting
takes effect with the next tuning or PID change.

Server peculiarities (quirks)
-----------------------------
Instead of a table of device names the plugin reacts to servers which deviate
from the standard:

 * Session ID with leading zeros which the server only accepts without the
   zeros (GSS.box, Telestar Digibit R1, Inverto IDL-400, Triax TSS 400): if a
   request is refused the plugin repeats it without the zeros.
 * addpids/delpids unknown (FRITZ!WLAN Repeater DVB-C): after a refusal the
   complete PID list is always sent for this session.
 * Retune inside a session refused (Fritz: TEARDOWN first): the plugin builds
   a new session at once, without waiting.
 * No pilot detection (GSS.box, Digibit, Inverto, Triax, Kathrein): for DVB-S2
   plts= is always sent (since 0.1.2).
 * Frontend lock is not reported (Fritz, Schwaiger): irrelevant, the lock is
   derived from the first TS packet.
 * 503 "no free frontend" (taken by another client): the server is blocked
   for 15 s for this kind of reception and the device moves to another server
   if there is one. Otherwise the retry runs every 5 s.

Detaching devices (since 0.5.0)
-------------------------------
  svdrpsend plug vtsatip DETA [N]     detach device N (no number: all)
  svdrpsend plug vtsatip ATTA [N]     attach again
Detached devices release their server stream and offer no sources any more,
e.g. so that another SAT>IP client can use the frontends. Devices which are
recording or being watched stay attached (answer 550). With -A / --detached
the plugin starts with all devices detached.

Setup menu (since 0.4.0)
------------------------
VDR Setup -> Plugins -> vtsatip. The values are kept in setup.conf and apply
at once, no restart is needed. Command line options are the defaults, stored
setup values take precedence.

  UPnP discovery               automatic / no / yes
                               (automatic: on if no -s is given; switching
                               starts or stops the search immediately)
  Transport mode               unicast / multicast / TCP
  VDR device N: server frontend
                               fixed frontend of the server (auto = the
                               server decides)
  Enable EPG scanning          use the devices for VDR's background EPG scan
  Disconnect idle streams      release the server frontend when idle
  Release unused stream after  seconds without use before the frontend is
                               released (5-3600, default 20)
  Request all PIDs             pids=all, see above
  Enable CI extension          shows, per VDR device, the CI slot, the CA IDs
                               ("0x1830,0x1843;0x0500"), the service IDs
                               ("4911,5301;") and "Restart stream on CI change"
  Tracing                      RTSP traffic in the syslog
  Statistics...                opens the statistics page (see below)
  Known SAT>IP servers         list with busy/free frontends

  The red key "Scan" starts a new UPnP search at once.

Statistics page (since 0.7.0): per device the server, channel, lock state,
signal strength and quality, transport (UDP/multicast/TCP), data rate and
total, RTP packets, lost and reordered packets (with the loss in percent),
the longest pause between two packets (now / since tuning), followed by the
known servers. The page refreshes once a second.

setup.conf keys (vtsatip.<name>): EnableCIExtension, CIRestart, CAIDs, SIDs,
CISlotDevice0..n, FrontendDevice0..n, Discovery (-1/0/1), EnableEITScan,
DisconnectIdle, IdleTimeout, AllPids, Transport (0/1/2), Trace.
Translations are in po/ (de_DE is provided); make creates the .mo files
(gettext is needed), make install installs them.

SVDRP
-----
  plug vtsatip INFO       state of all devices; per device also rtp= (received
                          RTP packets), lost= and reordered=, rate= (data rate
                          of the last 2 s), gap= (longest pause between two
                          packets in the last 2 s) and gapmax= (since tuning)
  plug vtsatip SCAN       search for servers now
  plug vtsatip ATTA|DETA  see above
  plug vtsatip TRAC 1     tracing on/off

Not included (deliberately)
---------------------------
 * The "CI TNR" extension (vendor specific, DVBViewer).
 * IPv6.
 * DVB-S2X specific values (additional roll-offs, higher-order APSK) and the
   DVB-C2 data slice / tuning frequency type (always sent as 0). DVB-T2 PLP and
   system id, S2 multistream via the stream id of the channel and all values
   VDR's channel data provides (see param.c) are sent.
 * CI/CAM operation has been implemented following the x_pmt/x_ci
   specification but could not be tested on real hardware yet. Reports are
   welcome.

Tuning tips
-----------
 * Realtime priority: VDR must be allowed to set SCHED_FIFO, e.g. with systemd:
       LimitRTPRIO=10   (and possibly AmbientCapabilities=CAP_SYS_NICE)
 * If packets are lost raise net.core.rmem_max, e.g. to 4194304, and set -r.
   The lost= counter in SVDRP INFO and the statistics page show whether it
   helped.
 * Video buffer underruns without lost packets: look at gap= and gapmax= in
   INFO (since 0.7.4). A pause of several hundred milliseconds means the server
   or the network delivered the stream late, nothing the plugin can fix. Small
   gaps and a steady rate= point to the video output instead.
 * A stream that delivers nothing for 5 seconds (10 seconds right after
   tuning) is set up again by the plugin (since 0.7.6); "the stream stalled"
   in the log shows that it happened. On a channel without signal some servers
   send nothing, so that message then repeats about every 10 seconds.
 * If the log says that the receive buffer is limited, raise net.core.rmem_max.

Developer information
---------------------
See README.developer for how the plugin works internally and how it talks to
VDR and the SAT>IP server.

